# MKUSR -- rebuild the DeSmet-built USER PROGRAMS on RealXV6 and check each
# against the /bin binary that is running the build.
#
#     cd /src/rt ; drun mkrt        (once, for the runtime objects)
#     cd /src/usr ; drun mkusr
#
# The user-program counterpart of /src/desmet's mkall: same fixpoint idea,
# one level out.  Each program is compiled here, linked against ../rt's
# objects with BIND -A, converted by d2a, and dcmp'd against /bin -- so
# IDENTICAL means the target reproduces its own userland byte for byte.
#
# -i../rt/ is what lets the source stay untouched: every program spells its
# include "UNIX.H", the prefix makes that ../rt/UNIX.H, and C88 takes options
# only AFTER the file name (C88.C loops from argv[2]), hence `c88 NAME -i...`
# and never `c88 -i... NAME`.
#
# Every cross-directory object spells its .O explicitly.  BIND appends .O
# only when the name has no dot at all (BIND.C nextpass: `if (*argat == '.')
# gotdot=1`), and "../rt/X" is full of dots, so ../rt/CRT0 would be opened
# verbatim and missed.  That is a clean "cannot open" on both sides now: it
# was silent here until dbind was relinked against DCRT0 (-1 on error) rather
# than CRT0 (the kernel's -errno).  That is dbind's OWN runtime -- not the
# ../rt/CRT0.O being linked below, which stays the -errno one because the
# programs built here are V6 programs.  See MKALL and ../rt/DCRT0.A.
#
# The link list is the V6-flavour one -- ULIB/UCOMPAT/CTIME/SETEXIT, not the
# tools' DCOMPAT/V6DOS/V6CHAIN -- matching the DOS cross-build's link tail.
# Objects are removed after each program: with -snapshot the disk is a
# throwaway overlay, but the free list still has to hold the whole run.
#
# The list is MKBIN's, in MKBIN's order: every program that bat compiles
# through XCC.BAT on the DOS side is rebuilt here on the target.  The other
# eight in /bin are the toolchain ports, and /src/desmet's mkall does those --
# so the two scripts between them account for all 45 programs the image
# carries.  The publish checks exactly that before it will cut a tree: a name
# in one list and not the other, or in neither, stops it.
#
# /init is the one dcmp not aimed at /bin.  The proto puts init at the root,
# where the kernel looks for it, so the comparison spells /init.

c88 INIT -i../rt/
dbind ../rt/CRT0.O INIT ../rt/SYSCALL.O ../rt/ULIB.O ../rt/PRINTF.O ../rt/CTIME.O ../rt/SETEXIT.O ../rt/UCOMPAT.O -A -L../rt/ -OINIT
d2a INIT.EXE init.new 1000
dcmp init.new /init
rm INIT.O INIT.EXE init.new

